WEBVTT

00:00:01.280 --> 00:00:06.760
My swatch has given me so much information
that I can now use to plan

00:00:06.793 --> 00:00:08.850
my hand knit blanket.

00:00:08.883 --> 00:00:12.090
Nope, I'm not knitting
this one on the machine.

00:00:12.123 --> 00:00:16.400
First, I don't have enough needles
and I don't want to knit panels.

00:00:16.520 --> 00:00:22.400
Next, this is going to be a get-out-
of-the-studio project just for fun.

00:00:24.400 --> 00:00:29.250
But the planning would be the same if I
was going to knit this on the machine.

00:00:29.283 --> 00:00:34.170
I want my like it to be 50
inches wide and 72 inches long.

00:00:34.203 --> 00:00:40.840
At my gauge, that means I need
to knit 250 stitches for 72 inches.

00:00:40.960 --> 00:00:44.770
When hand knitting, we don't have
to count rows like in machine knitting.

00:00:44.803 --> 00:00:49.280
And honestly, I'll probably
knit until I run out of yarn.

00:00:49.600 --> 00:00:57.700
Speaking of running out of yarn,
my swatch is 6 inches by 7.5 inches and it weighs 18 grams.

00:00:57.900 --> 00:01:05.290
I used the Knit It Now yarn
estimator and entered 6 by 7.5 and 18 grams.

00:01:05.323 --> 00:01:08.080
To knit this blanket, 50 inches by 72

00:01:08.113 --> 00:01:16.330
inches, I'll need about 1440 grams.

00:01:16.363 --> 00:01:20.960
My yarn comes in 100-gram balls,
so the 15 that I bought

00:01:20.993 --> 00:01:23.200
will be just right.

00:01:24.240 --> 00:01:26.730
I'm planning a garter stitch border.

00:01:26.763 --> 00:01:31.360
My swatch reminded me that the garter
stitch gauge is considerably wider

00:01:31.393 --> 00:01:34.210
and shorter than my stitch pattern.

00:01:34.243 --> 00:01:38.530
Can you see how the edge
dips on this side?

00:01:38.563 --> 00:01:41.930
I just plan to block this
out so it isn't so noticeable.

00:01:41.963 --> 00:01:44.850
This is just a blanket after all.

00:01:44.883 --> 00:01:50.170
But the top and bottom borders
are really flaring out.

00:01:50.203 --> 00:01:55.700
I've charted my 50-inch wide blanket
based on my stitch pattern gauge.

00:01:55.933 --> 00:01:59.840
To prevent the garter edges from flaring
out, I'll cast on about

00:01:59.873 --> 00:02:02.370
10% fewer stitches.

00:02:02.403 --> 00:02:06.080
I'll knit the border,
and then I'll increase back up

00:02:06.113 --> 00:02:12.720
to the required 250 stitches and continue
to knit my blanket in pattern.

00:02:12.800 --> 00:02:17.730
When I get to within about two inches
of the finished length, I'll decrease

00:02:17.763 --> 00:02:21.320
evenly across the row
and then finish the border

00:02:21.353 --> 00:02:25.290
in garter stitch over 225 stitches.

00:02:25.323 --> 00:02:29.330
By changing the stitch count,
I'll prevent this.

00:02:29.363 --> 00:02:35.210
I'm kind of embarrassed to share this,
but as a new knitter many, many moons ago,

00:02:35.243 --> 00:02:40.440
I didn't understand the relationship
between the seed stitch border and the

00:02:40.473 --> 00:02:43.650
beautiful cable panels of this project.

00:02:43.683 --> 00:02:48.040
Although this is a treasured family
heirloom, I cringe every

00:02:48.073 --> 00:02:50.360
time I use this blanket.

00:02:53.160 --> 00:02:57.370
Now, as I've been working with this stitch
pattern by hand, I realized that we

00:02:57.403 --> 00:02:59.410
could knit this on the machine.

00:02:59.443 --> 00:03:03.690
It's basically, a three
by three rib set up with the ribber.

00:03:03.723 --> 00:03:11.650
Knit two rows, transfer stitches between
the beds, knit two rows, and continue.

00:03:11.683 --> 00:03:16.370
For a smaller project, all those
transfers might not be too bad.

00:03:16.403 --> 00:03:18.250
But that's for another day.

00:03:18.283 --> 00:03:22.360
I need to get away from the studio and get
knitting on this promised

00:03:22.393 --> 00:03:23.800
blanket for my granddaughter.

